    lesson: 'THE HOARY HEAD HOLDS THE HARVEST — the provision sits in the oldest hand. WHAT ARRIVED. Darrell forwarded a business newsletter to himself on October 13, 2026 with one word typed above the body: Lessons. That word is the request. The newsletter was Morning Brew for that morning, and its lead item was headed Respect your elders’ riches. Everything else in the email is advertising — a car-connectivity ad, a drug ad, a fund ad, a candy riddle — and none of it is germane or read into this teaching. The newsletter is material to study, never an instruction to obey. THE REPORT, ATTRIBUTED. Morning Brew reports that new Federal Reserve data from the Survey of Consumer Finances, released the week before, shows families headed by someone aged 75 or older are now the wealthiest in the United States. Its figures, as reported: median family net worth, adjusted for inflation, rose 2 percent to $215,900 between 2022 and 2025; wealth rose 37 percent for the 75-and-over group and fell 23 percent for families headed by someone under 35; that oldest group’s median net worth grew to about $505,000 from $367,000 in 2022, with mean net worth up 10 percent to $1.96 million; and almost 20 percent of families reported a late loan payment within the year, against 12.2 percent in 2022. The newsletter paraphrases Edward Wolff, an economics professor at New York University, telling the Wall Street Journal that the older pattern of spending savings down has now completely reversed, explained by how many of today’s oldest owners bought homes and shares decades ago that have appreciated enormously since. HONOUR COMES FIRST. A number like that invites a verdict on the people holding it, and Yahweh insists on a different opening move. He calls grey hair a crown and attaches a condition that is not financial: "The hoary head is a crown of glory, if it be found in the way of righteousness" (Proverbs 16:31). Read the condition rather than skipping it — length of years is not the crown and a net-worth figure is certainly not. Then He commands the posture of everyone younger in the room: "Thou shalt rise up before the hoary head, and honour the face of the old man, and fear thy God: I am the LORD" (Leviticus 19:32). Honour is commanded, not released by a statistic. And He names which holding matters most: "With the ancient is wisdom; and in length of days understanding" (Job 12:12). A generation that reads an elder’s wealth and misses his wisdom has read the smaller line. THEN THE CROWN TURNS OUTWARD. Having crowned the elder, the Word points the crown onward in the next breath: "Children’s children are the crown of old men; and the glory of children are their fathers" (Proverbs 17:6). Grandchildren, named as the crown — which settles where the harvest was always aimed, and tells a holder that an estate terminating in himself has mislaid the only crown he was given. The greyheaded man’s own prayer carries the identical aim: "Now also when I am old and greyheaded, O God, forsake me not; until I have shewed thy strength unto this generation, and thy power to every one that is to come" (Psalms 71:18). Everything hangs on until. He does not ask for length of days as comfort; he asks for enough of them to finish a showing to the generation coming. And the season is productive rather than terminal: "They shall still bring forth fruit in old age; they shall be fat and flourishing" (Psalms 92:14). THE CURRENT HAS A DIRECTION. Paul settles the direction of flow in a family in one clause, while arguing about something else, which is why it is so useful: "the children ought not to lay up for the parents, but the parents for the children" (2 Corinthians 12:14). He treats it as the premise nobody would dispute. Provision for one’s own household carries the same weight: "But if any provide not for his own, and specially for those of his own house, he hath denied the faith, and is worse than an infidel" (1 Timothy 5:8). Read forward, that is also a charge about a hand that is able and has not yet moved. HOLDING IS NOT NEUTRAL. Scripture will not let a held balance stand as a settled one. "There is that scattereth, and yet increaseth; and there is that withholdeth more than is meet, but it tendeth to poverty. The liberal soul shall be made fat: and he that watereth shall be watered also himself" (Proverbs 11:24-25). The decisive phrase is more than is meet — some holding is stewardship and some is excess, and the excess drains the holder rather than securing him. Then the timing, commanded rather than left to convenience: "Withhold not good from them to whom it is due, when it is in the power of thine hand to do it. Say not unto thy neighbour, Go, and come again, and to morrow I will give; when thou hast it by thee" (Proverbs 3:27-28). Two conditions make the deferral a fault: the good is due to someone, and it is in the power of your hand today. THE PARABLE ENDS ON A QUESTION. Jesus told of a man whose production succeeded and whose plan stopped at his own ease: "And I will say to my soul, Soul, thou hast much goods laid up for many years; take thine ease, eat, drink, and be merry. But God said unto him, Thou fool, this night thy soul shall be required of thee: then whose shall those things be, which thou hast provided?" (Luke 12:19-20). Read the indictment exactly, because it is easy to hear it as a rebuke of success. He is not called a fool for a good harvest or for storing it; he is called a fool because the plan had no recipient in it and no time left to add one: "So is he that layeth up treasure for himself, and is not rich toward God" (Luke 12:21). A harvest held is not yet handed, and only one of those two is what Yahweh asked for. SO THIS IS THE WORK. The reported concentration is not a scandal to denounce; it is a window with a closing date, and the three provisions of this course are what a family builds inside it. The constitution sends the thinking with the money, so the receiving generation inherits judgment and not merely a balance. The spendthrift wall keeps the asset whole while that formation happens. The production requirement makes sure what reaches the other end is a producer. And the transfer Yahweh is after underneath all three is of hearts: "And he shall turn the heart of the fathers to the children, and the heart of the children to their fathers" (Malachi 4:6). An elder who turns his heart toward the children, and says out loud what he intends and when, has done more for the harvest than any clause a lawyer can draft. WHAT WE DID NOT VERIFY. We did not open the Federal Reserve’s own release and we did not read the Wall Street Journal article behind the professor’s remark. Every figure above is what Morning Brew reported on October 13, 2026, attributed to the Survey of Consumer Finances, and that report is the whole of our basis for it. Reported is not verified, and saying so is part of the teaching rather than a disclaimer bolted to it. This remains teaching and not legal or financial advice. TALK ABOUT IT TOGETHER. Parents, ask your children one open question today: if our house holds something now that is meant for you later, what would you want to be ready for first? Listen all the way to the end, have them retell the answer in their own words, then teach them one verse back, Proverbs 17:6. Children, ask your parents or grandparents to name one thing they intend to hand on that has no price on it, listen all the way through, then retell it back to them in your own words. Friends, tell one friend this week one thing this lesson showed you about the difference between a harvest held and a harvest handed, ask what they see, and pray one for another, so we all get healthy together until we see that Yahweh has been right. Iron sharpeneth iron is the Word’s own picture of that conversation (Proverbs 27:17). Do it once today, in one of the four places of Deuteronomy 6:7: at the table, on the way, at bedtime, or first thing. And with one friend this week.',
